{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_02ba9158e25820370169bb3b80d6048191ba655accad1a671a", "created": 1773878144, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Online Patient Portals (MyChart) for Downloading/Printing Medical Records\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on patients’ collection and organization of medical documentation—especially during cancer diagnosis and treatment—using online patient portals (e.g., MyChart) to access, download, and print key records for storage in a medical binder. The main functional chain described is: an online patient portal provides access to test results (including blood test results and cancer-related reports), the patient downloads or prints those records, and then adds them to an organized record set/binder for reference and second opinions. The entities are primarily patient-facing practices and information artifacts (records, test results, reports), connected through relationships emphasizing retrieval, portability, and organization rather than any adversarial or controversial activity. [Data: Entities (940, 211, 1515, 1533, 3726); Relationships (273, 1926, 7629, 2454, 2452)]\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Online patient portals are the enabling hub for accessing and exporting health information\",\"explanation\":\"The ONLINE PATIENT PORTAL is described as a convenient and recommended way for patients to view test results online and retrieve detailed records such as pathology reports and other medical information, with examples including MyChart. This positions the portal as the central access point that connects patients to their underlying medical documentation. [Data: Entities (940); Relationships (1926)]\\n\\nIn addition to viewing records, the portal supports exporting actions—specifically downloading and printing—so documents can be saved for personal reference and organized record-keeping. This export capability is a critical bridge between digital access and offline storage/organization. [Data: Entities (940, 1533, 3726); Relationships (273)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Using portals is framed as an intentional patient practice tied to record-keeping workflows\",\"explanation\":\"USING ONLINE PATIENT PORTALS is explicitly defined as the practice of accessing digital portals to view medical test results and health records, then downloading or printing those records to store in a medical binder or organized files. This frames portal usage not as passive viewing but as an active information-management workflow. [Data: Entities (211); Relationships (273)]\\n\\nThe relationships emphasize that portals provide access to test results and other records for viewing and export, reinforcing that the community’s focus is operational: obtaining copies of information for downstream organization and use. [Data: Relationships (273)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Downloading/printing records is the key operational step that turns digital results into portable documentation\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes both DOWNLOAD OR PRINT RECORDS and DOWNLOADING OR PRINTING RECORDS as actions the patient can take using online patient portals to obtain copies of test results and other records for inclusion in a medical binder. The presence of both entities underscores the importance of this step in the overall workflow. [Data: Entities (1533, 3726, 940)]\\n\\nThese actions are directly linked to organized storage, indicating that the intended outcome is portability and structured reference—supporting later consultations and continuity across providers or second opinions. [Data: Relationships (2450, 7627); Entities (1515)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The organized ‘records’ set is broad and includes cancer-specific documentation\",\"explanation\":\"RECORDS are defined as the patient’s medical documentation, including blood tests, imaging results, and reports about the specific cancer, and patients are recommended to copy, organize, and store these materials for reference and second opinions. This establishes the scope of what is meant by “records” in this community and why they matter. [Data: Entities (1515)]\\n\\nA specific relationship connects RECORDS to CANCER REPORTS, indicating that cancer-focused reporting/interpretation is a recommended component of the organized documentation set. [Data: Relationships (2454); Entities (1516, 1515)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Blood test results are highlighted as a specific record type accessed via portals for retention\",\"explanation\":\"BLOOD TEST RESULTS are identified as records and outcomes from the patient’s blood testing that the patient is advised to obtain, copy, and store in a medical binder, explicitly tying these results to ongoing medical reference. [Data: Entities (162)]\\n\\nThe data further links BLOOD TEST RESULTS to the ONLINE PATIENT PORTAL as the place where patients view and obtain these results for printing/downloading into the binder, demonstrating a concrete example of how a particular record type flows through the portal-to-storage process. [Data: Relationships (7629); Entities (162, 940)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Relationships emphasize a structured, patient-controlled documentation pathway rather than organizational risk or misconduct\",\"explanation\":\"Across the community, the relationships consistently describe recommended patient behaviors: portals provide access to test results, patients download/print records, and those records are stored/organized (e.g., in a medical binder). This pattern indicates a focus on personal health information management and preparedness for clinical interactions (including second opinions), not on legal disputes, fraud, or malicious technical activity. [Data: Entities (940, 211, 1515); Relationships (273, 2450, 7627)]\\n\\nWhile the community involves sensitive health information (test results and cancer reports), the provided data does not include claims about data m